{
 'title': 'How fossils are formed', 'context': 'A lecture in an introductory palaeontology course on the processes that turn living things into fossils.',
 'vocab': ['sediment', 'permineralisation', 'mould and cast', 'trace fossil', 'index fossil'],
 'script': [
  ('Lecturer', 'CA', "Good morning, everyone. Today we're going to look at how fossils are formed, and why the fossil record gives us such an incomplete picture of past life. The word fossil comes from a Latin word meaning 'dug up', and originally it was used for almost anything taken out of the ground. Today, we use it to mean any preserved evidence of life from the distant past, usually more than about ten thousand years old."),
  ('Lecturer', 'CA', "The first thing to understand is that fossilisation is extremely rare. When most plants and animals die, they simply disappear. Their bodies are eaten by scavengers, broken down by bacteria, or scattered and worn away by wind and water. Only a tiny fraction of all the living things that have ever existed have become fossils, and only a tiny fraction of those have been found."),
  ('Lecturer', 'CA', "So what conditions make fossilisation more likely? The most important is rapid burial. If an animal is quickly covered by sediment, such as mud, sand or volcanic ash, it's protected from scavengers, and the supply of oxygen is cut off, which slows down decay. That's why so many fossils come from places that were once at the bottom of seas, lakes or rivers, where sediment builds up steadily. The second factor is the presence of hard parts. Bones, teeth and shells are far more likely to survive than skin, muscles or leaves, so animals with skeletons are much better represented in the fossil record than soft-bodied creatures such as worms or jellyfish."),
  ('Lecturer', 'CA', "Let's look at the main ways fossils form. The most common process is called permineralisation. After burial, groundwater seeps through the sediment and into the tiny spaces inside bone or wood. This water carries dissolved minerals, such as silica or calcite, which slowly crystallise and fill these spaces, turning the original material into stone. Petrified wood, which can preserve the detail of tree rings, is formed in this way."),
  ('Lecturer', 'CA', "Sometimes, though, the original material dissolves completely, leaving a hollow space in the rock with the exact shape of the organism. This is called a mould. If that space is later filled by other minerals, the result is a cast, which is like a natural copy of the original. Many fossil shells are preserved in this way."),
  ('Lecturer', 'CA', "Not all fossils are the remains of bodies. Trace fossils are signs of an organism's activity, such as footprints, burrows, tooth marks and fossilised dung. These can tell us things that bones cannot. For example, long trackways of footprints suggest that some dinosaurs travelled in herds, and the distance between footprints can be used to estimate how fast an animal was moving."),
  ('Lecturer', 'CA', "There are also some rare cases of exceptional preservation. Small creatures such as insects and spiders have been trapped in sticky tree resin, which hardened into amber, preserving them in remarkable detail. In the frozen ground of Siberia, woolly mammoths have been found with skin, hair and even stomach contents. And at the La Brea tar pits in Los Angeles, thousands of animals, including sabre-toothed cats, became stuck in natural asphalt and were preserved."),
  ('Lecturer', 'CA', "Perhaps the most famous site of all is the Burgess Shale, high in the Rocky Mountains of Canada, which was discovered by the American scientist Charles Walcott in 1909. It's around five hundred million years old, and it preserves soft-bodied animals that would normally leave no trace at all, giving us a unique window into early animal life in the oceans."),
  ('Lecturer', 'CA', "Finally, fossils are not only useful for studying ancient life; they also help geologists date rocks. Certain species existed for a relatively short time but were widespread, so when they're found in a layer of rock, they show its approximate age. These are known as index fossils, and ammonites are a classic example. Next week, we'll look at how fossils are excavated and prepared in the laboratory."),
 ],
 'groups': [
  G('Questions 31–40. Complete the notes below. Write ONE WORD ONLY for each answer.', [
   Q('note', 'Most dead organisms are eaten by __________ or broken down by bacteria', 'scavengers', 'Their bodies are eaten by scavengers', 'Scavengers eat most bodies.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Rapid burial cuts off the supply of __________', 'oxygen', 'the supply of oxygen is cut off', 'Burial removes oxygen and slows decay.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Soft-bodied animals, e.g. worms and __________, are rarely preserved', 'jellyfish', 'soft-bodied creatures such as worms or jellyfish', 'Jellyfish are soft-bodied.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Permineralisation: minerals such as __________ or calcite fill spaces', 'silica', 'dissolved minerals, such as silica or calcite', 'Silica is one such mineral.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Petrified wood can preserve the detail of tree __________', 'rings', 'which can preserve the detail of tree rings', 'Tree rings can be preserved.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'A mould filled by other minerals becomes a __________', 'cast', 'the result is a cast', 'A filled mould becomes a cast.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Trackways suggest some dinosaurs moved in __________', 'herds', 'some dinosaurs travelled in herds', 'Trackways suggest herds.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Insects preserved in hardened tree resin, called __________', 'amber', 'which hardened into amber', 'Resin hardens into amber.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'At La Brea, animals were trapped in natural __________', 'asphalt', 'became stuck in natural asphalt', 'They were trapped in asphalt.', max=1),
   Q('note', '__________ fossils, such as ammonites, help to date rocks', 'index', 'These are known as index fossils', 'Index fossils date rock layers.', max=1),
  ]),
 ],
},
